INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "DOJ indictment and Senate social-media findings", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-003; SRC-004; SRC-002", "text": "DOJ indictment alleges that the Russian Internet Research Agency operated from 2014 to interfere with U.S. political processes, including the 2016 presidential election; received funding from Yevgeniy Prigozhin and Concord entities; created false U.S. personas and used stolen identities; operated pages/groups on divisive issues; hid Russian origins with U.S.-based infrastructure; pursued a strategic goal of sowing discord; by early-mid 2016 supported Donald Trump and disparaged Hillary Clinton; staged rallies, bought ads, solicited/compensated U.S. persons, and sought contact with unwitting Trump-associated individuals. The indictment identifies the translator project on YouTube, Facebook, Instagram, and Twitter, more than 80 employees by July 2016, a goal to spread distrust, Concord as primary funder, Project Lakhta, and a September 2016 monthly budget exceeding 73 million rubles / over $1.25m. Senate Volume II says IRA operatives masqueraded as Americans, used targeted ads, falsified news, self-generated content, and platform tools to reach tens of millions, polarize on societal, ideological, and racial differences, provoke real-world events, and covertly support Russia's favored candidate. Senate findings say the IRA sought to harm Clinton and support Trump, at Kremlin direction; emphasize race, African-American targeting, Blacktivist's 11.2m Facebook engagements, 66% of Facebook ads containing race-related terms, and 96% of YouTube content targeting racial issues/police brutality. The committee reports approximately $100,000 in Facebook/Instagram ads versus 61,500 Facebook posts, 116,000 Instagram posts, and 10.4m tweets, and found offline mobilization and interaction with unwitting Americans. Oxford/Graphika summary says campaigns reached tens of millions; over 30m users shared IRA Facebook/Instagram posts; organic posting was more far-reaching than ads; tactics included voter boycott/wrong procedures targeting African Americans, confrontation among extreme right-wing voters, and sensationalist/conspiratorial misinformation across the spectrum; activity increased after the election."}
